1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,900
No photograph of a living
Tyrannosaurus rex has ever existed.

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:06,110
No witness. No preserved muscle.

3
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:07,450
No skin sample.

4
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:10,800
No color chart. Almost all
of the soft tissue is gone.

5
00:00:10,820 --> 00:00:16,060
What's left is mostly rock. And yet, walk
into almost any natural history museum on

6
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:19,090
Earth, and you'll find a
Tyrannosaurus standing there.

7
00:00:19,110 --> 00:00:22,370
Fully fleshed, textured,
colored, mid-stride,

8
00:00:23,130 --> 00:00:26,430
looking back at you like someone
simply forgot to take the photograph.

9
00:00:26,450 --> 00:00:29,660
Here's the uncomfortable part. We've
gotten this badly wrong before.

10
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:34,420
In the 19th century, scientists working
on one of the first dinosaurs ever

11
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:40,360
reconstructed, Iguanodon, found a strange,
spike-shaped bone among the fossils and

12
00:00:40,380 --> 00:00:44,770
couldn't quite place it. Their best guess
was that it belonged on the animal's face,

13
00:00:44,790 --> 00:00:50,030
a horn, sitting on the nose. For years,
that's exactly how Iguanodon was drawn,

14
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:52,100
sculpted, and displayed to the public.

15
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:53,130
It wasn't stupidity.

16
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:56,230
It was the best possible
reading of incomplete evidence.
